---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';

// The showcase, kept as data so a new project is one entry rather than a
// hunt through markup. `count` is a real figure and must stay one: commit
// counts come from the GitHub contributors API, stars from the repo itself.
const building = [
  {
    name: 'nuthatch',
    count: '1,047 commits',
    href: 'https://nuthatch-indexer.com',
    blurb:
      'Be your own indexer. One Rust binary, one command, and a live indexed API in under two minutes. It follows the chain, seals segments to Parquet, serves SQL and MCP, and has no mandatory third-party data dependency. Built as a public good rather than a business.',
    meta: [
      { label: 'Rust', href: null },
      { label: 'nuthatch-indexer.com', href: 'https://nuthatch-indexer.com' },
      { label: 'source', href: 'https://github.com/nightswatchhq/nuthatch' },
    ],
  },
  {
    name: 'redstart',
    count: '104 commits',
    href: 'https://redstart-lang.com',
    blurb:
      'A language for authoring subgraphs, and the sexiest way to do it. Schema, manifest and mappings collapse into one typed source that compiles to AssemblyScript the canonical toolchain builds unmodified. The whole family of AssemblyScript footguns becomes unrepresentable: if it compiles, it works.',
    meta: [
      { label: 'Rust', href: null },
      { label: 'redstart-lang.com', href: 'https://redstart-lang.com' },
      { label: 'source', href: 'https://github.com/nightswatchhq/redstart' },
    ],
  },
  {
    name: 'yatr',
    count: '62 commits',
    href: 'https://yetanothertaskrunner.com',
    blurb:
      'The best task runner ever, and a single binary. Its cache is content-addressed and signed, so a shared cache entry can be trusted rather than hoped about, and it speaks REAPI so the remote execution you already run will talk to it.',
    meta: [
      { label: 'Rust', href: null },
      { label: 'yetanothertaskrunner.com', href: 'https://yetanothertaskrunner.com' },
      { label: 'source', href: 'https://github.com/cargopete/yatr' },
    ],
  },
];

const shipped = [
  {
    name: 'matchstick',
    count: '217 stars',
    href: 'https://github.com/LimeChain/matchstick',
    blurb:
      'Unit testing for subgraphs, in Rust and WebAssembly, written at The Graph in 2021. The ecosystem standardised on it and it is still maintained by other people, which is the outcome you want. 217 stars, 17 forks, and I have not touched it in years.',
    meta: [
      { label: 'Rust + WebAssembly', href: null },
      { label: 'The Graph, 2021', href: null },
      { label: 'source', href: 'https://github.com/LimeChain/matchstick' },
    ],
  },
  {
    name: 'graphcast, and subgraph-radio',
    count: '2022 - 2025',
    href: 'https://github.com/graphops/graphcast-sdk',
    blurb:
      'A P2P messaging protocol for The Graph indexer network, built on Waku and libp2p at GraphOps. Gossip, fault-tolerant delivery, and a radio pattern that let anyone add a message type without touching the core. Subgraph Radio was the first radio built on it, gossiping subgraph health between indexers.',
    meta: [
      { label: 'Rust, libp2p, Waku', href: null },
      { label: 'graphcast-sdk', href: 'https://github.com/graphops/graphcast-sdk' },
      { label: 'subgraph-radio', href: 'https://github.com/graphops/subgraph-radio' },
    ],
  },
  {
    name: 'On-chain data, and the pipelines under it',
    count: 'Sep 2026 -',
    href: 'https://dune.com/home',
    blurb:
      'On-chain data wizard at Dune. Before that, two years of Rust services behind an AI data platform at Fathom: multi-workspace monorepo, services, storage and control plane, and the dataops workflows that keep a model fed with something true.',
    meta: [
      { label: 'Rust', href: null },
      { label: 'dune.com', href: 'https://dune.com/home' },
      { label: 'the full history', href: '/resume/' },
    ],
  },
];
---

<Layout
  title="Petko (Pete) Pavlovski - senior Rust engineer"
  description="Senior Rust engineer in Sofia. Building nuthatch, redstart and yatr; contributor across The Night's Watch. Previously Matchstick and Graphcast at The Graph and GraphOps."
>

  <!-- HERO -->
  <section id="top">
    <div class="wrap hero-grid">
      <div>
        <span class="eyebrow rise rise-1"><span class="dot"></span>Sofia, Bulgaria · remote since 2019</span>
        <h1 class="display rise rise-2">I write the part<br>that has to stay up.</h1>
        <p class="lede rise rise-2">
          Rust, mostly. Indexers, languages, P2P protocols and the tooling
          around them. Most of it is still running.
        </p>
        <div class="btn-row rise rise-3">
          <a class="btn btn-primary" href="#building">See what I am building <span class="arrow">&rsaquo;</span></a>
          <a class="btn btn-ghost" href="/resume/">Read the resume</a>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="rise rise-3">
        <div class="term" role="img" aria-label="Terminal transcript. The first command counts my public repositories by language and prints 40 Rust, 13 JavaScript, 7 Python and 7 Dart. The second sums my commits across every repository in the Night's Watch organisation and prints 3199.">
          <div class="term-bar">
            <span class="term-dots" aria-hidden="true"><i></i><i></i><i></i></span>
            <span class="term-title">cargopete - zsh - 88×14</span>
          </div>
          <!-- Real output, run on 2 September 2026. Not a character is
               invented; the only liberty taken is colouring the counts. Both
               commands can be pasted into a shell and reproduced. If the
               numbers drift, rerun them and edit this panel rather than
               rounding it.

               This is a real pre element, not a stack of flex rows. `uniq -c` right
               aligns its counts, and Astro's compressHTML collapses leading
               whitespace everywhere except inside a pre, which quietly turned
               that column into a left-aligned lie. A pre also means the lines
               paste into a shell as written, which is the whole claim the
               caption makes. -->
<pre class="term-body" aria-hidden="true"><span class="pre-cmd">$</span> gh api users/cargopete/repos --paginate \
     --jq '.[]|select(.fork|not)|.language' \
   | sort | uniq -c | sort -rn | head -4
<span class="tout">  <span class="n">40</span> Rust
  <span class="n">13</span> JavaScript
   <span class="n">7</span> Python
   <span class="n">7</span> Dart</span>
<span class="pre-cmd">$</span> for r in $(gh api orgs/nightswatchhq/repos \
       --paginate --jq '.[].name'); do
    gh api repos/nightswatchhq/$r/contributors \
      --jq '.[]|select(.login=="cargopete")|.contributions'
  done | paste -sd+ - | bc
<span class="tout"><span class="n">3199</span></span>
<span class="pre-cmd">$</span> <span class="term-cursor">&#9608;</span></pre>
        </div>
        <p style="font-family: var(--mono); font-size: 0.66rem; letter-spacing: 0.08em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--text-muted); margin-top: 12px;">
          Real output, run on 2 September 2026. Paste either line into a shell.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- PROOF -->
  <section class="proof">
    <div class="wrap proof-grid">
      <div class="stat" data-reveal>
        <div class="n">3,199<span> commits</span></div>
        <div class="l">Across all 90 repositories in The Night's Watch. Nuthatch and Lodestar are 2,003 of them.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="stat" data-reveal>
        <div class="n">217<span> stars</span></div>
        <div class="l">On Matchstick, the subgraph testing framework I built for The Graph in 2021.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="stat" data-reveal>
        <div class="n">40<span> of </span>93</div>
        <div class="l">Public repositories in Rust. The rest are JavaScript, Python, Dart and a little OCaml.</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- BUILDING -->
  <section id="building">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head" data-reveal>
        <p class="kicker">Building now</p>
        <h2 class="display-sm">Three things I use every day.</h2>
        <p class="section-lede">
          All three exist because the tool did not, all three are one binary,
          and all three are public. None of them is a business.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="shelves">
        {building.map((p) => (
          <div class="shelf" data-reveal>
            <div class="shelf-head">
              <span class="shelf-num">{p.count}</span>
              <div class="shelf-body">
                <h3><a class="shelf-link" href={p.href} rel="external noopener">{p.name}</a></h3>
                <p class="shelf-note">{p.blurb}</p>
                <div class="shelf-meta">
                  {p.meta.map((m) => (m.href
                    ? <a href={m.href} rel="external noopener">{m.label}</a>
                    : <span>{m.label}</span>))}
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <hr class="divider" />

  <!-- THE NIGHT'S WATCH -->
  <section id="watch">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head" data-reveal>
        <p class="kicker">The Night's Watch</p>
        <h2 class="display-sm">Ninety repositories, and I have touched all of them.</h2>
        <p class="section-lede">
          A community organisation building the unglamorous half of The Graph:
          indexers, gateways, data services, judges and doctors. It is where
          most of my week goes.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="bento">
        <div class="cell c-feature" data-reveal>
          <div>
            <span class="tag tag--rust">Contributor since 2025</span>
            <h3><a href="https://github.com/nightswatchhq" rel="external noopener">nightswatchhq</a></h3>
            <p>
              Nuthatch and Lodestar are the two big ones, but the interesting
              work is the long tail: Dispatch, a decentralised JSON-RPC service
              on Horizon. Foghorn, a network-quality judge that catches indexers
              serving confident, well-formed garbage. Horizon-doctor, which turns
              a startup panic into an explained gate. Graphite, a Rust SDK for
              subgraphs.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="flow">
            <span class="chip">90 repos</span><span class="arrow">→</span>
            <span class="chip">3,199 commits</span><span class="arrow">→</span>
            <span class="chip chip--hot">all of it public</span>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="cell c-third" data-reveal>
          <span class="tag">Lodestar</span>
          <h3><a href="https://github.com/nightswatchhq/lodestar" rel="external noopener">956 commits</a></h3>
          <p>
            An indexer dashboard that became an indexer. It runs on nuthatch,
            which is the sort of thing that only happens if both are yours.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="cell c-third" data-reveal>
          <span class="tag">Learn The Graph</span>
          <h3><a href="https://learn-thegraph.com" rel="external noopener">graph-academy</a></h3>
          <p>
            A community learning site where every protocol number carries the
            date somebody last checked it, and the build goes red when one
            goes stale.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="cell c-third" data-reveal>
          <span class="tag">Also in there</span>
          <h3>Judges and doctors</h3>
          <p>
            Foghorn, horizon-doctor, swissindexingknife, indexer-gateway-auth.
            Small tools for operators, written because somebody was doing it by
            hand.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <hr class="divider" />

  <!-- SHIPPED -->
  <section id="shipped">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head" data-reveal>
        <p class="kicker">Shipped, and still running</p>
        <h2 class="display-sm">Work other people now maintain.</h2>
        <p class="section-lede">
          The measure of a tool is whether it survives you leaving. These did.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="shelves">
        {shipped.map((p) => (
          <div class="shelf" data-reveal>
            <div class="shelf-head">
              <span class="shelf-num">{p.count}</span>
              <div class="shelf-body">
                <h3><a class="shelf-link" href={p.href} rel="external noopener">{p.name}</a></h3>
                <p class="shelf-note">{p.blurb}</p>
                <div class="shelf-meta">
                  {p.meta.map((m) => (m.href
                    ? <a href={m.href} rel="external noopener">{m.label}</a>
                    : <span>{m.label}</span>))}
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- CLOSE -->
  <section class="closer" id="contact">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div data-reveal>
        <p class="kicker">Contact</p>
        <h2 class="display-sm">Mail is the fastest way.</h2>
        <p class="section-lede">
          Not looking for work. Always happy to talk about indexers, subgraph
          tooling, or anything in the list above.
        </p>
        <div class="cta-row">
          <button class="copyline" data-copy="pavlovskipetko@gmail.com"
                  aria-label="Copy my email address to the clipboard">
            <span><span class="prompt">$</span> pavlovskipetko@gmail.com</span>
            <span class="copy">copy</span>
          </button>
          <a class="ghost" href="/resume/">Read the resume <span class="arrow">&rsaquo;</span></a>
        </div>
        <div class="links">
          <a href="https://github.com/cargopete">github.com/cargopete</a>
          <a href="https://github.com/nightswatchhq" rel="external noopener">The Night's Watch</a>
          <a href="mailto:pavlovskipetko@gmail.com">pavlovskipetko@gmail.com</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

</Layout>
